# Your own ontology and everything derived from it. `data/repo` is a git repository in its own right —
# it is the thing to back up, and it is not this repository's to carry. A copy of the example that
# ships here is in examples/.
/data/

# Written by install.sh from .env.example, and it holds this machine's uid/gid.
.env

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.DS_Store

# The second backbone's data, for anyone trying a link out locally. Its own repository, the way
# data/ is — docker-compose.peer.yml, docs/PEERING.md.
/data-b/

# The demonstration domain: three more backbones and a second exchange. Built by
# examples/seed-demo.sh from examples/back-office, so what is versioned is the forty lines that make
# them different from it rather than five copies of the same worked example.
/data-vendor/
/data-audit/
/data-depot/
/data-partner-ix/

# The retrieval experiment (eval/). "What is generated is ignored; what generates it is not" was the
# rule here, and it was half wrong. It holds for `crowd.py`, which imports nothing but the standard
# library and writes the same 352 documents every time — the generator really is the artefact there.
# It does not hold for `generate.py`, which asks an LLM to write the base corpus. Nobody can run it
# and get the same 861 documents back, so with the corpus ignored, the fingerprint every run record
# is stamped with could not be reproduced by an outside reader **or by us on a fresh checkout**. The
# corpus is now tracked: 4.8MB of markdown against a study that is otherwise unverifiable.
#
# Still ignored below: things derived from the corpus rather than the corpus itself — embeddings,
# section indexes, probe results, logs — all of which any checkout can rebuild from what is here.
# The manifests are not derived from the corpus — they are what says which documents are *sections*,
# the signposts this study added, which are walkable but must stay out of the retrieval pool. Drop
# them and every section page gets indexed, which changes the fingerprint and every retrieval number
# with it. 316KB, and without them a clean clone cannot recompute anything.
/bench/sections.json
/bench/embeddings*.json
/bench/factors.csv
/bench/*.log
bench/.probe.lock
/bench/probe-questions.json
/bench/probe2-questions.json
/bench/probe-results.json
/bench/probe-results-fusion.json
/bench/probe2-results.json
/bench/harden-state.json
/bench/crowd-fusion.json
/bench/crowd-curve*.json

# Runs whose corpus can no longer be identified. The 13:31 rebuild on 2026-09-20 overwrote all 353
# extension documents, so a run from that morning cannot be checked by anyone — the corpus it saw is
# gone. Kept on disk as history; not tracked, because a number nobody can verify sitting beside
# numbers anybody can makes the directory look like more evidence than it holds. eval/runs/README.md
# says what they were and where their conclusions went.
/eval/runs/superseded/

# The census runner's own scratch: a log, and the per-walk helper it writes next to the reports.
/eval/runs/census.log
/eval/runs/census-overlay.log
/eval/runs/walks-census/.walk.sh
